      "title": "Rules don't explicitly tell you when you lose Natural Flanking Fire?",
      "query": "Rules don't explicitly tell you when you lose Natural Flanking Fire?: I imagine it's as simple as you are no longer in the conditions for it and therefore don't have it.  The 17.4 section only refers to Flanking Fire.  It's specifying that specific rule.  Not the Natural one as far as I can tell.  \n\nFF is lost when the flanking group plays a movement but that would be an odd condition for NFF since it wouldn't necessarily break the conditions needed for NFF.  I guess then can a group currently at NFF play, say, a lateral movement card, thereby not changing range chit, thereby maintaining the conditions for NFF and still enjoy the benefits of double fire power?  Which now has me remembering discussions on do you double first or halve first and if halve first do you still round down before doubling.... \n\nWait a minute... upon further inspection... 17.3 (NFF) still speicifcally calls it Flanking Fire.  So the 17.4 losing conditions do apply to NFF?  Removing wire and failing to Ford a stream would make you lose NFF?  But how would one regain NFF as you're still in the exact same conditions to qualify for it?  But... the condition for NFF is moving forward to RR4 (chits 6) from RR5.  That seems to be it.  Moving forward.  So stopping at RR4 (6 on chits) you lose it as you no longer are moving? I know over thinking it but...",
