      "ground_truth_answer": "Flanking is a complicated rule.\n\nGaining Natural Flanking is a result of moving from RR5 to \"RR6\" per 17.3.. (I prefer \"RR6\" to \"RR4 but behind the target group\".)\n\nLosing any form of Flanking occurs under 17.4X, specifically:\n-  Flanking group has a movement or Wire played on it.  (but not Terrain--needed to get doubled FP without 1/2FP for moving.)\n-  Flanked group has terrain, even if rejected.\n-  Flanked group has a Movement played on it, even temporarily to remove Wire or failed Ford attempt.\n-  Front group eliminated or changes ID chit/completes a lateral group \"transfer\".\n\nGaining flanking (either naturally or via a Flank card) places a Flank Fire chit.  Losing Flanking removes the Flank Fire chit.  The presence of the ***chit*** determines doubled FP for flanking.\n\nOnce, natural flanking is lost  it would be difficult to regain. (retreat to RR5 and advance back to RR6) But, when in a natural Flanking position (i.e. RR6) (whether Flanking was lost or not), that group can Encircle on a later turn (if the Front group is at RR2 to the Flanked group), by playing a sideways Movement cards on the Flanking group.\n\nAnd always double/halve all FP before rounding down.",
