      "ground_truth_answer": "14.5 If, in the course of a multi-card discard, two or more Sniper and/or\nMinefield attacks are played on the same group resulting in the\nelimination of the only target in that group, the discarding player may\nalternate his selection of a target group for any discards yet to be\nresolved.\n\nThe wording sure seems to suggest if, say, you're Russia and you go nuts with 4 \"in lieu of discards\" and it's 3 Snipers and a minefield you're supposed to pre-designate where each one is going.  If not then what's the point of this rule?  This also makes no sense if you first did the snipers and eliminated the only group that had a movement card on it therefore making your minefield discard ineligible.  So... what's this rule really talking about?\n\nThe point of the rule is you have only have the right to retarget a sniper or Minefield if it's target group ends up empty before resolution.  You cannot do that with other active discards (Terrain, Wire)\n\nIn a discard that includes wires & 2 snipers, you to predesignate where all three cards go.  Now, let's say you put them same single man group:\n-  The wire will go on the group.\n-  If the first sniper eliminates the group, you can retarget the 2nd sniper and the Wire (and any terrain or movement cards) will be sent to the discard pile.\n-  If the second sniper eliminates the group, the Wire and any terrain or movement will go to the discard pile.\n\nThis rule prevents per errata (see 7.3 & 7.32) for example resolving a Sniper and NOW that a man is pinned, putting the Wire on that group.  Or the Sniper NOW eliminated the group, you could NOW send the Wire to a different group.",
